findings.description:
Rat.SetString, which JSON decoding uses for every ledger amount, accepts exponent notation, e.g. "1p9999999" (2^9999999, 11 bytes). Decoding allocates the full number. The size cap is measured only by re-serializing (WithinMessageBound → json.Marshal → decimal conversion), and VerifyPayment/PaymentID RatString the amount first. So the cost is paid before any bound rejects the message:
- at JSON decode on every endpoint (proposals are decoded before proposer authentication, and decode() does not require a JSON Content-Type);
- on handler goroutines (signature check);
- on the single event loop (admitPayment).
The same applies to account amounts in a peer-served checkpoint (no body limit at all) and to governance Rats.
